\section{A Common Source Language}

Common parentheses did not make early Lisp implementations compatible.
A program could use familiar names for list operations yet depend on
different calling conventions for unevaluated arguments, a different
interpretation of free variables, or system-specific input and output.
Anthony C. Hearn's 1969 \textit{Standard LISP} proposed a restricted
source language for Lisp 1.5 and compatible systems. A system-specific
preprocessor translated it into the host dialect. The symbolic algebra
system REDUCE provided a concrete portability target: the source had
to run under several existing systems, without requiring every host to
be organized alike.

The later \textit{Standard Lisp Report}, by Jed Marti, Hearn, Martin L.
Griss, and C. Griss, identifies two limitations of the earlier approach.
Some function results had been left too loosely defined for portable
clients to rely on them. Also, when interpretive definitions were stored
in host-specific form, a portable compiler or editing tool could not
assume a common representation for the definitions it inspected. The
revision therefore tightens the specified behavior and asks more of the
target implementation. As Guy L. Steele Jr.\ and Richard P. Gabriel's
\textit{The Evolution of Lisp} explains, Portable Standard Lisp then
attacked a different problem: porting the implementation itself. Its
compiler produced abstract assembly and used machine-dependent
\emph{c-macros} to select target instructions. Sharom Hekmatpour's
\textit{Lisp: A Portable Implementation} took another route by building
the compact Kernel dialect in C. A portable implementation of Kernel
is not thereby a conforming implementation of Common Lisp.

\section{Common Lisp and Its Specification}

Steele's first edition of \textit{Common Lisp: The Language} (1984)
documented an agreed design descended chiefly from the Maclisp family;
it was not the ANSI standard. The 1990 second edition records many
X3J13 decisions and explicitly disclaims official committee status.
Committee participant Masayuki Ida's \textit{History of Lisp
Standardization during 1984--1990} describes the tension between
codifying practice and adopting new interfaces, particularly CLOS.
Julian Padget and colleagues' \textit{Desiderata for the Standardisation
of LISP} shows why a larger reconciliation effort had to accommodate
users and dialects outside the Common Lisp project. ANSI Common Lisp
was issued in 1994 as X3.226; the separately standardized ISLISP is
not another name for it.

The standard provides a contract for sources and their observations,
not a common binary format, identical diagnostics or one optimization
strategy. In \textit{ANSI Common Lisp}, Paul Graham describes packages
as a mechanism for maintaining separate symbol namespaces. Their effect
begins at read time: identically spelled tokens read in two different
packages need not be the same symbol. Packaging is therefore a
portability concern before the compiler has even started to analyze
expressions. An application must also distinguish fixed guarantees from
implementation-dependent choices and from situations for which the
standard supplies no behavioral promise.

Harlan Sexton's \textit{Foreign Functions and Common Lisp} compares
several vendor interfaces, illustrating a place where source portability
stops. For instance, a library may use portable Common Lisp to decode
a payload but need an implementation-specific adapter to import it
from C. Keep that adapter narrow and test marshalling, ownership, and
the lifetime of any object that the collector might otherwise move.
Changing the adapter should not require changing the decoder.

\section{Worked Portability Test: Reader and Loader}

Load these definitions in a clean Common Lisp image:

\begin{verbatim}
(defpackage :counts.a (:use) (:export #:total))
(defpackage :counts.b (:use) (:export #:total))
(assert (not (eq 'counts.a:total 'counts.b:total)))
(assert (string= (symbol-name 'counts.a:total)
                 (symbol-name 'counts.b:total)))
\end{verbatim}

The first assertion tests identity, and the second ensures a difference
in spelling did not make the first test pass accidentally. Neither test
relies on the printer choosing a particular package prefix. Put the
package declarations before any client file that uses the external
names. Test a source load and a compile-then-load on each implementation,
in a fresh image for each workflow; compile artifacts locally rather
than assuming one implementation can read another's compiled files.
A client that works only after previous interactive definitions have
populated the relevant packages has not passed the test.

This workflow separates reader, compiler, and loader requirements.
The reproducible-build proposal in Chapter~44 of \textit{The Modern
Compiler Dossier} makes the analogous distinction between declared
semantic inputs and ambient build state. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-156}
applies it specifically to code that must run while a Lisp file is
being compiled.
