\section{Testing Multiple Values Without Relying on the Printer}

A portability harness should compare observations the source contract
actually specifies. Consider a small ANSI Common Lisp library
whose search routine returns an index and a Boolean indicating
success. Test it through a caller using \texttt{multiple-value-list},
which captures both the number and order of returned values.
Restrict the cases to integers and the standard truth values, so
the harness can encode them without relying on package prefixes,
object addresses, or vendor printer settings. The inputs are the
same source release and fixture arguments, built locally in each
implementation. The output is a semantic mismatch report separate
from failures to load or compile. This is a modern test, not a
claim that these facilities belong to the earlier Standard Lisp
portability effort.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input implementations $I$, source release $S$, fixtures $F$
  \Output load failures and semantic mismatches
  \State $D\gets$ empty list
  \ForAll{implementation $i$ in $I$}
    \ForAll{mode $m$ in source-load and compile-load}
      \State $p\gets\Call{FreshImage}{i}$
      \Comment{No prior library definitions}
      \State \Call{Prepare}{$p,S,m$} or record load failure and skip
      \ForAll{fixture $(a,w)$ in $F$}
        \State $v\gets\Call{CaptureValues}{p,a}$
        \Comment{Count secondary values too}
        \If{$v\ne w$}
          \State append $(i,m,a,w,v)$ to $D$
        \EndIf
      \EndFor
    \EndFor
  \EndFor
  \State \Return $D$
\end{sudocode}

Search for $7$ in the vector $[7,9]$. The expected value
tuple is $(0,\mathsf{true})$, and a missed search for $5$
has expected tuple $(0,\mathsf{false})$. Suppose a compiled
build of the wrapper silently keeps just the primary value.
Both calls now yield $(0)$, and the harness reports two
mismatches in compile-load mode even if the source-load mode
passes. Merely comparing the first value would have passed
both runs. In Common Lisp $0$ is true in a test position,
so a caller treating the index as the Boolean would also
fail to distinguish these inputs.

The comparison invariant is that tuple arity and each position
are checked according to specified equality for the fixture
domain. Comparing printed REPL transcripts could accidentally
test prompt formatting or whether secondary values are shown.
Use a fixture for zero returned values when that case is legal:
zero values are not one value of \texttt{nil}. Do not compare
binary artifacts from different vendors; the contract being
tested is the interface seen by the caller.
